+          <h3 id="quiz-title">Who said it? 🕵️</h3>
+          <p class="game-output" id="quiz-quote" aria-live="polite">Press “Deal a quote” to get a mystery hot take.</p>
+          <p id="quiz-answer" aria-live="polite">No points, just bragging rights and evidence.</p>
+          <div class="game-actions"><button id="quiz-deal" type="button">Deal a quote</button><button id="quiz-reveal" type="button" disabled>Reveal the name</button></div>
+        </section>
+      </div>
+      <div class="team-grid">
         <article class="member-card">
           <div class="profile-head">
             <div class="portrait-wrap"><img class="portrait" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Portrait of Soumya Bani" loading="lazy"></div>
             <div class="profile-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Clouds, but make them useful</p><h3>Soumya Bani</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science student at Paderborn University, Germany.</p><p class="label">Interests (the toolchain)</p><ul class="interests"><li>DevOps</li><li>AI</li><li>Cloud Computing</li><li>AI-enhanced workflows</li></ul></div>
           </div>
           <div class="details">
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Fun fact / current side quest</h4><p>I'm building a multi-agent system for incident management and automation, exploring how AI agents can collaborate to investigate incidents and streamline operational workflows.</p></section>
             <section class="detail"><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p>AI is changing how we write and maintain software, and it's already making developers more productive. However, generating code is only one part of software engineering. Understanding the system, making architectural decisions, debugging, and ensuring reliability and security still require strong engineering fundamentals. I see AI as a powerful engineering assistant, not a replacement for engineering knowledge.</p></section>
             <section class="detail future"><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p>I think software engineering will move towards more autonomous, AI-driven workflows, where developers orchestrate agents that can build, test, deploy and monitor applications. The role of engineers will increasingly shift from writing every line of code to designing systems, defining constraints and validating outcomes. Cloud, DevOps and AI will become even more interconnected.</p></section>
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Hot take, fresh from the pipeline</h4><p class="hot-take">“The future belongs to engineers who know what to automate and, more importantly, what not to.”</p></section>
           </div>
         </article>
 
         <article class="member-card">
           <div class="profile-head">
             <div class="portrait-wrap"><img class="portrait" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Portrait of Sarpesh" loading="lazy"></div>
             <div class="profile-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Wires: 1 · Code: 0</p><h3>Sarpesh</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering, 3rd semester, Paderborn University.</p><p class="label">Interests (the hardware bits)</p><ul class="interests"><li>Embedded systems</li><li>Power electronics</li><li>Automation and control</li><li>AI for engineering</li></ul></div>
           </div>
           <div class="details">
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Fun fact / debugging scoreboard</h4><p>I spend more time debugging wiring than code, and the wiring usually wins.</p></section>
             <section class="detail"><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p>AI coding agents are great for boilerplate, quick scripts and explaining unfamiliar code. But they still make confident mistakes, so every change needs to be reviewed and tested. Right now they are a strong assistant, not a replacement for understanding the system.</p></section>
             <section class="detail future"><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p>Engineers will spend less time typing code and more time specifying, reviewing and verifying what agents produce. Domain knowledge, for example in electrical and embedded systems, will matter even more, because someone has to judge whether the generated solution is actually correct and safe.</p></section>
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Hot take, voltage checked</h4><p class="hot-take">“An AI that writes code you can't verify isn't saving time; it's just moving the bug to production faster.”</p></section>
           </div>
         </article>
 
         <article class="member-card">
           <div class="profile-head">
             <div class="portrait-wrap"><img class="portrait" src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Portrait of Ranjith" loading="lazy"></div>
             <div class="profile-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Backend with a side of LiDAR</p><h3>Ranjith</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering (Signal &amp; Information Processing) at Universität Paderborn.</p><p class="label">Interests (the moving parts)</p><ul class="interests"><li>Python backends</li><li>Django &amp; FastAPI</li><li>Data pipelines</li><li>Computer vision on real hardware</li><li>RL for drone navigation</li></ul></div>
           </div>
           <div class="details">
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Fun fact / privacy by design</h4><p>I'm building a kitchen staff-tracking system that uses LiDAR instead of cameras – so nobody gets filmed.</p></section>
             <section class="detail"><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p>AI is great at boilerplate, glue code and getting unstuck, and it has made me noticeably faster. But it's confidently wrong often enough that you still need to understand every line it writes. The bottleneck has shifted from typing code to reviewing it, and most teams haven't adjusted for that yet.</p></section>
             <section class="detail future"><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p>Engineers will spend more time specifying, reviewing and testing than writing code by hand, with agents handling much of the implementation. The valuable skills will be system design, knowing the domain, and judging whether the output is actually correct. Codebases will grow faster than anyone can read them, so testing and observability will matter more than ever.</p></section>
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Hot take, no camera required</h4><p class="hot-take">“AI won't replace engineers, but it will expose the ones who never understood the code they were copying.”</p></section>
           </div>
         </article>
 
         <article class="member-card">
           <div class="profile-head">
             <div class="portrait-wrap"><img class="portrait" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Portrait of Namit" loading="lazy"></div>
             <div class="profile-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Vibes, but with architecture</p><h3>Namit</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 5th semester.</p><p class="label">Interests (the human hobbies)</p><ul class="interests"><li>Football</li><li>Chess</li><li>Vibe coding random projects just for fun</li></ul></div>
           </div>
           <div class="details">
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Fun fact / left field</h4><p>I'm left-handed, like only about one in ten people.</p></section>
             <section class="detail"><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p>AI has changed which skills matter in software engineering. It is unmatched at writing code, so writing code is no longer where an engineer adds the most value. What counts now is thinking like a product manager, deciding what is worth building and why, and thinking like an architect about how the pieces fit together.</p></section>
             <section class="detail future"><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p>I think this shift will only go further. AI will write most of the code, and engineers will be the people who decide what gets built and how the system is designed. In ten years, a software engineer will be closer to a product manager and an architect than to a programmer.</p></section>
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Hot take, email edition</h4><p class="hot-take">“AI is making us lose our edge: we can't even reply to an email anymore without pasting it into ChatGPT first.”</p></section>
           </div>
         </article>
 
         <article class="member-card">
           <div class="profile-head">
             <div class="portrait-wrap"><img class="portrait" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Portrait of Gagan" loading="lazy"></div>
             <div class="profile-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Signal, information &amp; strong opinions</p><h3>Gagan</h3><p class="study">M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering at Paderborn University, specializing in signal and information processing. B.Sc. in Electronics and Communication Engineering.</p><p class="label">Interests (outside the terminal)</p><ul class="interests"><li>Bike rides</li><li>Listening to music</li><li>Fitness</li></ul></div>
           </div>
           <div class="details">
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Fun fact / no plot twist</h4><p>I hate anime.</p></section>
             <section class="detail"><h4>AI in software engineering today</h4><p>Software engineering has already changed a lot: most code is now written by agents, and humans act largely as validators. Still, coding agents struggle to understand requirements and to produce well-structured code (for example, following OOP principles), so human supervision remains necessary.</p></section>
             <section class="detail future"><h4>Software engineering in 5–10 years</h4><p>Software engineering will change rapidly. Coding agents are getting better at generating complex code and understanding user requirements from only a few prompts. Humans will increasingly move into a supervisory role, reviewing the work agents do, which means faster development timelines and fewer human resources.</p></section>
             <section class="detail fun-fact"><h4>Hot take, supervisor's cut</h4><p class="hot-take">“Developers won't be replaced by AI, but developers who don't supervise it well will be replaced by those who do.”</p></section>
           </div>
         </article>
       </div>
       <p class="tiny-note">Footnote from the humans: no bios, fun facts or hot takes were generated by an autocomplete. (The layout, however, has had assistance.)</p>
     </section>
 
     <section class="shell outlook" id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-title">
       <div class="outlook-panel">
         <div><p class="eyebrow">The group project answer</p><h2 id="future-title">AI gets the keyboard. Engineers keep the judgement.</h2></div>
         <div>
           <p class="team-verdict"><strong>Our shared view:</strong> AI is already useful for producing code, handling boilerplate, speeding up workflows and helping people get unstuck. In the next 5–10 years, agents will likely do more of the implementation—and may build, test, deploy and monitor whole applications—while engineers spend more time setting direction, designing systems, specifying constraints, reviewing, testing and validating results.</p>
           <p>We agree that engineering fundamentals and domain knowledge still matter: confident output can be wrong, poorly structured, unreliable or unsafe. As code gets generated faster, verification, testing, observability and knowing what <em>not</em> to automate become more important, not less.</p>
           <p><strong>Where we differ:</strong> Namit sees AI as already unmatched at writing code and expects it to write most code; Gagan says most code is already agent-written and humans largely validate it. Soumya, Sarpesh and Ranjith stress its strengths as an assistant today, alongside today’s mistakes and limits. The shared destination is similar; the “how far along are we?” marker is in different places.</p>
           <p>Translation: fewer keystrokes, more judgement—and nobody gets to ship a mystery diff just because the bot sounded confident.</p>
         </div>
       </div>
     </section>
   </main>
 
   <footer><div class="shell"><span>Built by an agent, supervised by humans.</span><span>Agentic SE Lab · Paderborn University · Five humans, one file</span></div></footer>
